.Dd $Mdocdate: August 23 2026 $
.Dt FUGUVM 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fuguvm
.Nd install and manage OpenBSD virtual machines
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm fuguvm
.Op Fl hq
.Op Fl Fl vm Ar name
.Op Fl Fl project Ar dir
.Op Fl Fl emulate
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
installs and manages
.Ox
virtual machines under QEMU.
It downloads the installation image,
creates the disk image,
and answers the installer over the serial console.
It then controls the machine's lifecycle,
guest access,
and console automation.
You need only one command to go from nothing to a machine that accepts
.Xr ssh 1 .
A second run of the command does nothing.
.Pp
The
.Ar arch
directive of a VM configuration selects the guest architecture:
.Cm amd64
or
.Cm arm64 .
The default is
.Cm arm64 .
The value selects the QEMU binary,
the machine type,
the firmware,
the installer answers,
and the image-cache key.
A disk belongs to one architecture for its whole life:
after a change of the directive,
rebuild the VM with
.Cm destroy
and
.Cm up .
.Pp
The host does not have to run
.Ox .
A host that runs QEMU is sufficient,
and
.Nm
selects the accelerator to match the host.
Thus a Linux or Darwin workstation can build,
test,
or reproduce a bug on a real
.Ox
system without an
.Ox
machine on the desk.
.Pp
.Nm
operates on a project:
a directory that has an
.Pa .fuguvmrc
configuration file and an
.Pa .fuguvm/
state directory.
The
.Cm init
command creates them.
If you do not give
.Fl Fl project ,
.Nm
searches up from the current directory for an
.Pa .fuguvmrc
file.
The directory that holds this file is the project root.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl Fl vm Ar name
Operate on the named VM.
The default comes from the
.Ar default_vm
directive,
or it is
.Dq default .
VM definitions are in
.Pa .fuguvm/vms/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .conf .
.It Fl Fl project Ar dir
Use
.Ar dir
as the project root,
and do not search for it.
.It Fl q , Fl Fl quiet
Do not show informational output.
.It Fl Fl emulate
Use TCG software emulation,
not hardware acceleration.
Without this flag,
.Nm
selects the accelerator automatically.
It selects KVM or HVF only when the host machine runs the
instruction set of the guest.
KVM serves Linux hosts with a writable
.Pa /dev/kvm ,
and HVF serves Darwin hosts.
It selects TCG in every other case.
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Show the usage message.
.El
.Pp
These commands are available:
.Bl -tag -width "expect script"
.It Cm init Op Ar dir
Initialize a project in
.Ar dir
(default: the current directory).
The command creates
.Ar dir
if it is absent.
It also creates the
.Pa .fuguvmrc
project configuration,
the
.Pa .fuguvm/
directory with the
.Pa vms/
and
.Pa state/
subdirectories,
and a default VM configuration.
The command is idempotent:
it does nothing if the project is already initialized.
.It Cm up Op Fl Fl no-cache
Make sure that the VM runs.
The command downloads the installation image,
creates the disk image,
and starts the VM,
as necessary.
The command is idempotent.
.Pp
The configuration selects one of three install modes.
Without an origin directive,
.Cm up
installs with the shipped expect installer.
With
.Ar autoinstall Ar file ,
it installs from an
.Xr autoinstall 8
response file:
a local HTTP responder serves the file to the guest on the loopback
address,
the guest fetches it through the QEMU gateway,
and the file answers every installer question.
The responder replaces each
.Ql @PROXY_URL@
token in the file with the guest URL of the mirror proxy.
QEMU runs with
.Fl no-reboot
for that installation,
so the reboot at the end of the install cannot start a second one.
With
.Ar base_disk Ar path ,
.Cm up
installs nothing:
it publishes the named image as a cache entry one time,
and overlays the working disk on it.
The two directives contradict each other.
.Pp
If the installed-image cache is on,
.Cm up
uses both sides of that cache.
If there is no disk image,
.Cm up
creates one as an overlay on a cached base that matches,
and boots the installed system directly.
This skips the miniroot download and the installer.
After an installation,
.Cm up
publishes the newly installed disk as a new cached base.
The cache operation is best effort:
a failure causes a warning,
and the VM starts,
with an installation from scratch or with a standalone disk.
.Pp
.Fl Fl no-cache
skips the restore and the save for this run.
This causes a fresh installation and does not change the cache.
The flag overrides the
.Ar image_cache
directive.
Use it to debug the installer itself.
.It Cm down
Stop the VM gracefully.
.It Cm destroy
Stop the VM and delete its disk image.
.It Cm start
Start the VM in the background.
.It Cm stop Op Fl f , Fl Fl force
Stop the VM.
With
.Fl f ,
stop it with force.
.It Cm status Op Ar key
Write the VM status to standard output,
as one
.Ar key : value
line for each key,
in sorted key order.
The lines are data,
so
.Fl Fl quiet
does not suppress them.
The key set is stable:
.Ar accel , arch , bind_address , console_port , disk_exists ,
.Ar installed , name , pid , proxy_url , ssh_port ,
and
.Ar state .
A value can be empty,
and an empty value means
.Dq not now :
a stopped guest has no
.Ar pid ,
and no port for a directive of
.Cm auto .
.Ar proxy_url
is the proxy URL as the guest reaches it,
and it is empty while the proxy does not run.
The port changes between runs,
so a consumer reads the value here and must not write it in a file.
.Pp
With
.Ar key ,
write the bare value of that one key,
with no key name and no colon.
Thus a script reads one port without a text filter.
An unknown
.Ar key
causes an exit with code 2,
and the diagnostic names every valid key.
.Cm disk info
follows the same
.Ar key : value
contract on standard output.
.It Cm ssh Oo Fl Fl Oc Op Ar command Op Ar argument ...
Open an interactive SSH session to the VM as root,
or run one remote command and exit with its exit code.
Authentication uses the SSH agent.
.Pp
With arguments,
each argument is one word of the argument vector.
The tool quotes every word.
Thus the remote shell performs no splitting,
no expansion,
and no globbing,
and the words arrive exactly as you wrote them.
Write
.Fl Fl
before any word that starts with a hyphen:
the option parser reads such a word as an option of the tool,
and it exits with code 2.
A caller that wants a remote shell asks for one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm ssh -- sh -c 'cd /src && make regress'
.Ed
.Pp
The remote command gets no terminal and no standard input,
and its output arrives when the command ends.
The interactive form keeps the standard input of the caller.
.It Cm put Oo Fl Fl mode Ns = Ns Ar octal Oc Ar local remote
Copy the local file or directory
.Ar local
into the running guest,
at the absolute path
.Ar remote .
.Ar remote
is never a container:
.Ql put src /root
writes the content of
.Pa src
into
.Pa /root ,
and it adds no
.Pa src
component.
The bytes move over the SFTP channel.
.Pp
The tool writes each file to a temporary name beside its destination,
and then publishes every file with
.Xr mv 1 .
A failure removes every temporary file.
Thus a failed run leaves no partial destination file.
A directory copy is not atomic as a whole:
a failure can leave files that an earlier batch published.
.Pp
.Fl Fl mode
sets the mode of each written file,
as 3 or 4 octal digits.
Without the option,
each file keeps its local permission bits,
masked with 0777,
so no setuid bit and no setgid bit arrives.
The option does not apply to a directory:
.Ql mkdir -p
gives each directory the mode that the remote umask allows.
.Pp
The tool refuses a source with a symbolic link,
a device node,
a socket,
or a fifo,
and a file above 64 MiB,
before it writes one byte.
.It Cm get Ar remote local
Copy one regular guest file at the absolute path
.Ar remote
to the host file
.Ar local .
The tool reads the whole file first,
and then writes
.Ar local
atomically,
mode 0644.
Thus a failure leaves no partial local file.
One call copies one file:
the tool lists no remote directory.
.Ar local
must not be an existing directory.
The command needs a Fugu release with
.Ql Fugu::SSH->read_file ;
with an older release it names the needed release and exits with
code 1.
.It Cm console
Attach the terminal of the operator to the VM serial console,
with
.Xr telnet 1 ,
at the connect address of the guest:
the
.Ar bind_address ,
or
.Li 127.0.0.1
when the ports bind to
.Li 0.0.0.0 .
The guest never closes the console.
End the session with the telnet escape key,
.Ql Ctrl-\&] ,
and then
.Ic quit .
The tool restores the terminal on every exit path.
.Pp
The console takes one client.
An installation and
.Cm expect
each hold the same port,
so do not attach while one runs.
.It Cm expect Ar script Op Ar argument ...
Run an
.Xr expect 1
script against the VM console.
.It Cm wait Op Fl Fl timeout Ns = Ns Ar seconds
Block until the VM accepts SSH connections
(default timeout: 120 seconds).
.It Cm cache Cm list | clear Op Fl Fl stale
Manage all data under
.Ar cache_dir :
the installed-image cache that
.Sx IMAGE CACHE
describes,
and the downloads that the built-in proxy keeps for the installer.
.Cm list
shows one line for each cached image
.Pq key, size, creation time, snapshot count .
It puts a mark on the entry that the invoked VM's configuration
derives at this time.
Then it shows the proxy's downloads by
.Ox
version,
and one
.Ql Distfiles
line with the size of the distfile tree and the
.Ar distfile_cache
cap.
The line stays absent while the tree is empty.
.Cm clear
removes each image and each download,
the distfile tree included.
.Fl Fl stale
keeps only the image that the invoked VM derives,
and only the downloads for the
.Ox
.Ar version
that it installs.
It keeps the distfile tree,
because a distfile carries no version and a refill is expensive,
and it re-applies the
.Ar distfile_cache
cap.
Both forms also remove temporary trees that an interrupted save leaves
behind.
.Pp
This removal is the only limit on the downloads.
Their key is the
.Ox
version alone,
and it has no input in common with the image keys.
Each pattern that the proxy caches has a version scope.
Thus,
after a
.Ar version
change,
the file sets of the previous version stay in the cache until you
remove them,
and nothing reads them again.
.Pp
The scope of
.Fl Fl stale
is one VM:
the VM that
.Fl Fl vm
names.
Each VM has its own
.Ar version
and
.Ar disk_size
key inputs.
Thus a removal for one VM removes bases that a different VM can use.
It also removes the downloads for the
.Ox
version that a different VM installs.
.Pp
.Cm clear
does not remove an entry while a VM in this project runs on it.
It gives a warning when a removal orphans the disk of a stopped VM
.Pq rebuild it with Cm destroy No and Cm up .
.Cm clear
cannot examine the VMs of other projects that share the same
.Ar cache_dir ,
and the two checks do not apply to them.
.It Cm mirror Cm fetch Ar file
Download one file of the release of the invoked VM,
verify it under the release key,
store it in the proxy cache,
and write the cached path to standard output,
where a script can read it.
The scope comes from the manifests:
the release scope when the
.Pa SHA256
of the architecture directory names the file,
and the source scope otherwise.
.Ql fuguvm mirror fetch ports.tar.gz
is the call that a consumer makes;
the guest then reads a verified ports tree from the cache,
over http,
through the proxy.
See
.Sx MIRROR CACHE .
.It Cm mirror Cm verify
Verify every cached file of the version and the architecture of the
invoked VM against the two signed manifests.
The command writes one scope-and-name line for each file that failed
and one summary line with the three counts
.Pq ok, failed, unknown .
It removes each file that failed,
in its own scope only,
because a file that fails a digest must not stay in a cache that a
later run reads.
A cached manifest whose signature fails counts as failed too,
and the command removes it with its signature.
A file of a scope with no proven manifest reads as unknown.
It does not remove an unknown file:
a name that no manifest holds is not a failure,
and
.Pa index.txt
is such a name on every mirror.
The command exits 1 when one file failed,
and 0 otherwise.
It is idempotent:
a second run over a clean cache removes nothing and exits 0.
.It Cm snapshot Cm save | restore | rm Ar name
.It Cm snapshot Cm list Op Fl Fl names
Manage named snapshot layers over the cached base image.
Use them to cache guest states that
.Nm
does not know about
.Pq usually the result of a provisioning script .
See
.Sx IMAGE CACHE .
.Pp
.Cm save
flattens the working disk into the cache,
under the base image that the disk uses.
.Cm restore
replaces the working disk with a new overlay on
.Ar name ,
and reseeds the VM state that the snapshot records.
Stop the VM before both operations:
a live overlay is not consistent.
.Cm save
also needs an installed VM with a disk that is built on a cached image.
You cannot snapshot a standalone disk
.Pq from Fl Fl no-cache No or Ar image_cache no .
.Cm restore
needs no disk and no state,
and it can run on a fresh checkout before the first
.Cm up .
.Pp
A snapshot that is absent or unresolvable causes an exit with code 11,
not 1.
Thus a script can see the difference between this and a real failure:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fuguvm snapshot restore deps-$hash || provision_from_scratch
.Ed
.Pp
The scope of
.Cm list
and
.Cm rm
is the cache entry that the current configuration derives.
.Cm list Fl Fl names
writes bare snapshot names to standard output,
one name on each line,
for scripts.
Without the flag,
the output goes to standard error,
the same as all other informational output.
.It Cm image Cm export Ar path Op Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Cm qcow2 | raw
Write the installed base image of the invoked VM as a full-disk image
at
.Ar path .
The source is the base image of the cache entry that backs the
working disk;
a snapshot layer resolves to the same entry.
A standalone disk
.Pq from Fl Fl no-cache No or Ar image_cache no
exports the working disk itself.
The default format is
.Cm qcow2 ,
and
.Cm raw
writes the sparse raw form.
Stop the VM first:
a live overlay is not consistent,
and the command refuses a running VM with exit code 5.
An existing
.Ar path
causes exit code 1,
because the command must not overwrite an image that an operator
published.
The write goes through one temporary sibling and one rename,
so a failure leaves no partial file behind.
The report on standard output follows the
.Ar key : value
contract of
.Cm status ,
with the keys
.Ar bytes , format , key , path
and
.Ar source .
.It Cm disk Cm check | repair | info
Manage the VM disk image.
.Cm check
does a check of its integrity.
.Cm repair
repairs errors.
Stop the VM before you run
.Cm repair .
.Cm info
shows the image details.
.It Cm help
Show the usage message.
.El
.Sh IMAGE CACHE
An installation of
.Ox
under software emulation takes tens of minutes.
Thus
.Nm
keeps the result:
a clean, compacted copy of the disk.
.Nm
makes the copy immediately after the installation is complete.
Later runs use the copy as the backing image of a throwaway overlay.
With a warm cache,
.Cm destroy
and then
.Cm up
are a cheap factory reset.
.Pp
Entries are under
.Ar cache_dir ,
adjacent to the proxy cache that holds the miniroot and the install sets:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<cache_dir>/proxy/...                            miniroot, install sets
<cache_dir>/installed/<key>/base.qcow2           mode 0400, compacted
<cache_dir>/installed/<key>/meta.json            mode 0600
<cache_dir>/installed/<key>/snapshots/<n>.qcow2  mode 0400
<cache_dir>/installed/<key>/snapshots/<n>.json   mode 0600
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar key
is
.Ar version Ns - Ns Ar arch Ns - Ns Ar hash .
The
.Ar hash
part is a truncated SHA-256 over all inputs that shape an installed
disk,
and the inputs follow the install mode.
The default mode hashes the
.Ox
version,
the architecture,
.Ar disk_size ,
the mode,
the contents of the
.Pa install.exp
installer script,
and the contents of
.Pa share/fuguvm/cache-generation .
The autoinstall mode hashes
.Pa autoinstall.exp
instead of
.Pa install.exp ,
and the contents of the response file join the hash:
two response files give two entries.
The import mode hashes no script and no
.Ar disk_size :
no script installed the image,
and an overlay inherits the virtual size of its base.
Thus an imported entry survives a change to a shipped expect script,
and every guest of a project derives the same key for one
.Ar base_disk .
The memory and port settings do not shape the disk,
and the hash excludes them.
Thus a change to them hits the same entry.
.Pp
An imported entry is write-once,
like every other entry,
and its snapshots work the same way.
.Nm
publishes the
.Ar base_disk
file into the cache with one
.Xr qemu-img 1
conversion,
one time for each host,
and it never writes to the source file.
.Pp
.Pa share/fuguvm/cache-generation
is a generation counter.
Increase the number in the file when the procedure that drives the
installer changes outside
.Pa install.exp .
This makes the cached images invalid.
Keep only the bare number in the file:
the hash includes the whole file.
.Pp
Entries are immutable and write-once.
.Nm
builds each entry whole in a temporary directory,
and then publishes the entry with a rename of that directory.
Thus a reader never sees the base image of one installation adjacent to the
metadata of a different installation.
.Pp
A snapshot is an immutable, named layer over the base image of an
entry.
.Cm snapshot save
creates the snapshot from a stopped VM.
.Nm
flattens each snapshot onto
.Pa base.qcow2 .
Thus each snapshot is a direct child of the base,
and the initial backing of the working disk has no effect:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
base.qcow2  <-  <name>.qcow2  <-  disk.qcow2
.Ed
.Pp
Thus no snapshot is the parent of a different snapshot,
and the removal of one snapshot cannot orphan a different snapshot.
The snapshots are inside the entry.
Thus each operation that makes a base invalid
.Pq a new key, or Cm cache clear
also makes the snapshots of that base invalid.
.Sh MIRROR CACHE
The built-in proxy caches what an installation and a ports build fetch
from
.Lk https://cdn.openbsd.org :
the file sets,
the packages,
the syspatch sets,
the kernels,
the miniroot images,
the checksums,
the source tarballs of a release
.Pq Pa ports.tar.gz , src.tar.gz , sys.tar.gz , xenocara.tar.gz ,
and,
with a
.Ar distfile_cache
cap above zero,
the
.Ox
distfile tree under
.Pa /pub/OpenBSD/distfiles/ .
The distfile pattern matches the path on any mirror host:
.Lk https://cdn.openbsd.org
carries no distfile tree,
and
.Lk https://ftp.openbsd.org
does.
The cache admits nothing else,
and it caches no https body:
only a guest fetch over plain http through the proxy reaches it.
.Pp
Every tree except the distfiles carries a version in its path,
and the version prune of
.Cm cache clear Fl Fl stale
bounds it.
A distfile belongs to a port and not to a release,
so no version bounds that tree.
The
.Ar distfile_cache
cap bounds it instead:
the proxy evicts the oldest file first,
by modification time,
after each distfile store.
The default cap is 0,
which turns the distfile cache off,
because an unbounded tree in a home directory is the failure to
avoid.
A consumer that wants the cache sets a size in its
.Pa .fuguvmrc .
.Pp
The trust chain has five links,
and the tool owns the first two:
.Bl -column "ports.tar.gz and the tarballs" "signature and key" -offset indent
.It Em content Ta Em signature Ta Em verifier
.It sets, kernels, miniroot Ta Pa SHA256.sig No of the arch dir Ta host and guest installer
.It Pa ports.tar.gz No and the tarballs Ta Pa SHA256.sig No of the version dir Ta host
.It a package Ta its own signify signature Ta Xr pkg_add 1 in the guest
.It a syspatch set Ta its own signature Ta syspatch in the guest
.It a distfile Ta the digest in Pa distinfo Ta Ql make checksum in the guest
.El
.Pp
The host verifies each download under the base key of the release,
with
.Xr signify 1 ,
before the file enters the cache.
The version names the key file:
7.8 gives
.Pa openbsd-78-base.pub .
The tool reads the key from
.Ar signify_dir
when the directive is set,
then from
.Pa /etc/signify/ ,
and then from the shipped
.Pa share/fuguvm/signify/
tree,
which holds the key of the default release.
A guest of an older release needs
.Pa /etc/signify/
or
.Ar signify_dir .
One key serves both manifests of a release,
and the tool serves no snapshot release:
a snapshot carries the key of the next release.
.Pp
The guest verifies its own sets:
the installer of a numbered release carries the release key in
.Pa bsd.rd .
With
.Ar verify yes
the install therefore stops on the
.Dq Continue without verification?
prompt with exit code 9,
because the prompt reports a broken mirror.
With
.Ar verify no
the tool downloads without a proof and the installer waives its
check,
with one warning on each side.
A distfile verifies in the guest too:
the host verified the ports tree,
the ports tree holds each
.Pa distinfo
digest,
and
.Ql make checksum
refuses a distfile that does not match.
.Pp
A ports build in the guest needs two lines of guest configuration,
and the consumer owns both.
First,
.Ev http_proxy
must name the
.Ar proxy_url
value of
.Cm status ,
because
.Xr ftp 1
in the guest reads that variable.
Second,
the ports tree must fetch each distfile from the
.Ox
distfile mirror,
.Lk http://ftp.openbsd.org/pub/OpenBSD/distfiles/ ,
over http,
because an upstream https site never reaches the cache.
.Xr bsd.port.mk 5
holds one user setting for that route:
.Ar SITE_BACKUP ,
the list of sites that the fetch tries after the primary sites.
An https primary site fails through the proxy,
the fetch falls back to
.Ar SITE_BACKUP ,
and that fetch fills the cache.
The fetch appends the distfile path to each entry,
the
.Ar DIST_SUBDIR
component of the port included,
and the mirror stores its distfiles under the same subdirectories:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm ssh -- sh -c \e
	"echo 'SITE_BACKUP = http://ftp.openbsd.org/pub/OpenBSD/distfiles/' \e
	>> /etc/mk.conf"
$ proxy="$(fuguvm status proxy_url)"
$ fuguvm ssh -- sh -c \e
	"cd /usr/ports/devel/gmake && env http_proxy=$proxy make fetch"
.Ed
.Pp
The proxy port changes between runs,
so read
.Ar proxy_url
from
.Cm status
on each run,
and do not write it in a file.
A distfile that the
.Ox
mirror does not carry never enters the cache;
a guest snapshot after the fetch saves that download,
and
.Cm put
of a host copy is the other route.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".fuguvm/vms/name.conf" -compact
.It Pa .fuguvmrc
Project configuration:
.Ar cache_dir ,
.Ar state_dir ,
.Ar default_vm ,
.Ar image_cache ,
.Ar qemu_version ,
.Ar bind_address ,
.Ar verify ,
.Ar signify_dir ,
.Ar distfile_cache .
.It Pa .fuguvm/vms/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .conf
Per-VM configuration:
.Ar name , arch , version , memory , disk_size , ssh_port ,
.Ar console_port , bind_address , autoinstall , base_disk ,
.Ar root_password_file , verify , signify_dir .
.It Pa .fuguvm/state/
Runtime state
.Pq PID files, sockets, disk images .
.It Pa ~/.cache/fuguvm/
Default cache directory:
downloaded installation images and the installed-image cache.
.It Pa share/fuguvm/cache-generation
Generation counter for the installed-image cache.
.It Pa share/fuguvm/signify/
The signify public keys of the supported releases.
.El
.Pp
.Ar image_cache Cm yes | no
.Pq default Cm yes
switches the installed-image cache on or off.
.Nm
reads the directive from the project
.Pa .fuguvmrc
or the global
.Pa ~/.fuguvmrc ,
with the project first.
The directive can also be in a
.Ar vm
block.
.Fl Fl no-cache
on
.Cm up
overrides all of them.
.Pp
.Ar ssh_port
and
.Ar console_port
take a decimal number from 1 to 65535,
or the word
.Cm auto .
With
.Cm auto ,
.Cm up
and
.Cm start
take a free host port from a fixed range, and record it.
Each range holds 100 ports.
It starts at the default port of its directive
.Pq 2222 for SSH, 4444 for the console .
The probe does not take a fixed port that a VM declaration of the
project holds, whether that guest runs now or not.
Read the recorded port with
.Cm status .
The record dies with the run:
.Cm stop
clears it,
and a later
.Cm start
resolves again.
An exhausted range causes an exit with code 1.
.Cm expect
and
.Cm wait
against a stopped guest with a directive of
.Cm auto
also fail with exit code 1,
because such a guest has no port.
.Cm ssh ,
.Cm put ,
.Cm get
and
.Cm console
refuse every stopped guest first,
with the same code.
.Pp
.Ar bind_address
names the host address of every forwarded port,
as one IPv4 address in dotted-decimal form.
The default is
.Li 127.0.0.1 .
A host name is invalid.
The directive can be in a
.Ar vm
block,
in the project
.Pa .fuguvmrc ,
or in the global
.Pa ~/.fuguvmrc ,
with that precedence.
.Pp
.Ar qemu_version
pins the version of the QEMU system binary,
as one or more dot-separated decimal numbers.
.Cm up
and
.Cm start
run the binary with
.Fl Fl version
and compare component by component,
over the components that the directive names:
.Li 9.0
accepts
.Li 9.0.4
and refuses
.Li 9.1.0 .
A mismatch,
an absent binary,
and output with no version in it,
each cause an exit with code 3.
Without the directive,
.Nm
checks nothing and does not run the binary.
.Nm
reads the directive from the project
.Pa .fuguvmrc
or the global
.Pa ~/.fuguvmrc ,
with the project first.
.Pp
A line that the configuration grammar does not accept is an error that
names the file and the line, and
.Nm
exits with 3.
The parser never skips a line it did not understand: a typo that a
parser ignores is a setting that silently does not apply, and the
operator finds out weeks later from the behavior of the VM.
.Pp
.Ar verify Cm yes | no
.Pq default Cm yes
switches the mirror verification on or off.
With
.Cm yes ,
.Nm
verifies each download under the release key before the file enters
the cache,
and the guest installer verifies its own sets.
.Xr signify 1
must then exist on the host,
and a run without it fails with a diagnostic that names the command
and the package.
The directive can be in a
.Ar vm
block,
in the project
.Pa .fuguvmrc ,
or in the global
.Pa ~/.fuguvmrc ,
with that precedence.
.Pp
.Ar signify_dir
names the directory of the signify public keys,
before
.Pa /etc/signify/
and the shipped keys.
The value is a path:
a leading tilde expands,
and a relative path resolves against the project root.
A value that is not a directory is a configuration error.
.Pp
.Ar distfile_cache
sets the size cap of the distfile tree,
as a byte count or a number with a
.Cm K ,
.Cm M
or
.Cm G
suffix
.Pq 1024-based, either letter case .
The default is 0,
which turns the distfile cache off.
The directive is a project or global setting:
the distfile tree is one tree under
.Ar cache_dir ,
and each guest of a project shares it,
so a per-guest cap would have no meaning.
See
.Sx MIRROR CACHE .
.Pp
A value for
.Ar image_cache
or
.Ar verify
that is neither yes nor no,
and a
.Ar distfile_cache
value that is not a size,
each give a warning and fall back to the default.
An unrecognized spelling must not silently mean the opposite.
.Pp
.Ar autoinstall
names an
.Xr autoinstall 8
response file,
.Ar base_disk
names an existing full-disk image
.Pq qcow2 or raw ,
and
.Ar root_password_file
names a file whose first line is the root password of the image.
Each value is a path:
a leading tilde expands,
and a relative path resolves against the project root.
The file must be readable when the configuration loads.
The response file is an artifact of the operator,
and
.Nm
validates no answer in it:
.Xr autoinstall 8
owns that grammar.
Outside the default install,
.Ar ssh_pubkey
needs
.Ar root_password_file ,
so
.Nm
can install the key over a password login.
A guest with no
.Ar ssh_pubkey
must trust the key of the operator already.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with one of these values:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent -compact
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
General error.
An
.Cm image export
whose target exists,
whose parent directory is absent,
or whose guest has no installed disk,
and a
.Ar base_disk
file that is absent at a cache miss,
also give this code.
So do a failed download,
a failed signature,
a failed digest,
a
.Cm mirror verify
that found one bad file,
and
.Ar verify yes
on a host with no
.Xr signify 1 .
.It 2
Invalid arguments.
An unknown
.Cm status
key,
an
.Cm image
call with no action,
no path,
or an unknown
.Fl Fl format
value,
and a
.Cm mirror
call with no action,
an unknown action,
or no file,
also give this code.
.It 3
Configuration error, or not in an
.Nm
project.
An unknown
.Ar arch
value,
an absent QEMU binary for the architecture,
an invalid
.Ar bind_address ,
.Ar ssh_port ,
.Ar console_port
or
.Ar qemu_version
value,
and a QEMU version that does not match the pinned
.Ar qemu_version
also give this code.
So do a
.Ar signify_dir
that is not a directory,
an absent public key for the
.Ar version
on a
.Cm mirror
call
.Pq the same miss on Cm up No fails the image, with code 1 ,
an absent or unreadable
.Ar autoinstall ,
.Ar base_disk
or
.Ar root_password_file
file,
.Ar autoinstall
together with
.Ar base_disk ,
.Ar base_disk
with
.Ar image_cache no ,
and
.Ar ssh_pubkey
without
.Ar root_password_file
outside the default install.
.It 4
VM not found.
.It 5
The VM runs.
An
.Cm image export
of a running VM also gives this code.
.It 7
Timeout in the wait for the VM.
.It 9
Expect script failed,
the install and autoinstall scripts included.
The installer prompt
.Dq Continue without verification?
under
.Ar verify yes
also gives this code,
because the prompt means that the mirror data is wrong.
.It 11
Snapshot not found.
.El
.Pp
The
.Cm ssh
command with a
.Ar command
argument exits with the exit code of the remote command,
once it connects.
A remote exit code can read like a tool code:
a remote command that exits with 4 looks like
.Dq VM not found .
The diagnostic on standard error tells the two apart,
because the tool logs each failure of its own.
The four connection verbs
.Pq Cm ssh , put , get , console
exit with 1 for a guest that does not run,
and
.Cm put
and
.Cm get
exit with 1 for a failed transfer.
They exit with 2 for a bad argument count,
a relative remote path,
or a bad
.Fl Fl mode
value.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Initialize a project and boot the default VM:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm init
$ fuguvm up
$ fuguvm wait --timeout=300
.Ed
.Pp
Run a command in the VM:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm ssh -- uname -a
OpenBSD openbsd-default.my.domain 7.8 GENERIC arm64
.Ed
.Pp
Copy a source tree in,
run a build step,
and copy the report back out:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm put src /root/src
$ fuguvm ssh -- sh -c 'cd /root/src && make regress > report.txt'
$ fuguvm get /root/src/report.txt report.txt
.Ed
.Pp
Operate on a VM other than the default:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguvm --vm minimal up
.Ed
.Pp
Define an amd64 VM in
.Pa .fuguvmrc
and verify its architecture:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vm "openbsd-amd64" {
	arch         amd64
	version      7.8
	memory       2048
	disk_size    8G
	ssh_port     2222
	console_port 4444
}

$ fuguvm --vm openbsd-amd64 up
$ fuguvm --vm openbsd-amd64 ssh -- uname -m
amd64
.Ed
.Pp
Declare a fleet of two guests with automatic ports,
start one,
and read its SSH port:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
vm "scenario-1" {
	ssh_port     auto
	console_port auto
}
vm "scenario-2" {
	ssh_port     auto
	console_port auto
}

$ fuguvm --vm scenario-1 up
$ fuguvm --vm scenario-1 status ssh_port
2222
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr expect 1 ,
.Xr signify 1 ,
.Xr ssh 1 ,
.Xr telnet 1
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility was first available in 2025.
Its initial purpose was to run the test suite of a daemon against a
real
.Ox
guest.
It became more general from there.
